# Copy to a file called `.env` at the REPO ROOT (not in this folder) and fill in.
# `.env` is git-ignored. Nothing in this file is a real value.
#
# These three are used by the scripts in scripts/ and 05_Test_Evidence/ only.
# The workflows themselves never read them: this n8n instance runs with
# N8N_BLOCK_ENV_ACCESS_IN_NODE=true, so $env throws inside a Code node. Runtime
# configuration lives in the `lp_config` data table instead, written by
# LP-00 Setup and Seed. See 03_Technical_Design/DESIGN.md section 6.

# Your n8n instance, no trailing slash.
N8N_API_URL=https://n8n.example.com

# Settings -> n8n API -> create an API key. Used to deploy workflows and to read
# the data tables from the test runner.
N8N_API_KEY=PUT_YOUR_N8N_API_KEY_HERE

# The shared secret every inbound webhook checks, sent as the `X-LP-Token`
# header. It must match the n8n credential named
# "LP Webhook Token (X-LP-Token)" (type: HTTP Header Auth).
# Generate one: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
LP_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=PUT_A_LONG_RANDOM_STRING_HERE


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT in this file, on purpose
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Odoo url / database / user / password  -> the `lp_config` data table, written
#   by LP-00. They differ per environment and the demo sandbox rotates them
#   every few hours, so a file is the wrong home for them.
#
# Gmail OAuth and the OpenRouter key     -> n8n credentials, referenced by NAME
#   in the workflow JSON ("Gmail account", "OpenRouter account"). The exported
#   JSON in 02_Workflows/ carries the names and no ids and no values, which is
#   what makes it safe to publish.
